te 80% of the main job rate. So, 80% of 20 is $16, which is the same. So, same result. Therefore, 15 hours at $16 is correct. So total is 840. Therefore, the answer is $840. I think that's right.\n\nBut let me think again to make sure there's no mistake in interpretation. The problem says he works 30 hours at his main job and half that much in his second job. Wait, \"half that much\". If \"half that much\" refers to half the time? If so, then as per my previous calculation, 15 hours. If \"half that much\" refers to half the amount? Then he works half the amount at the second job. The problem says \"half that much\". The phrasing is \"half that much\". So if you have two different jobs, and he works 30 hours at main job, and then \"half that much\" is 15 hours at second job. So, that seems correct. Because \"half that much\" is equivalent to half the time. So, yes, 15 hours. Therefore, my calculation seems okay.\n\nAnother way to think about it: second job is 20% less, so 80% of $20. Which is $16 per hour. So, 15 hours * 16 is $240. Adding to the first job's $600, total $840. Seems correct. I think that's the answer.\n\n**Final Answer**\nJames earns \\boxed{840} dollars per week.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how much James earns per week, we need to calculate his earnings from both his main job and his second job separately and then sum them.\n\n---\n\n### **Main Job:**\nJames works 30 hours at his main job, earning $20 per hour.\n\n$$\n30 \\, \\text{hours} \\times \\$20/\\text{hour} = \\$600\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Second Job:**\nHe works half of the hours he works at the main job, which is 30 hours. So, he works 15 hours at his second job.\n\nThe second job rate is 20% less than the main job, which means it is 80% of the main job's rate.\n\n$$\n80\\% \\times \\$20/\\text{hour} = \\$16/\\text{hour}\n$$\n\nTherefore, earnings from the second job:\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\text{hours} \\times \\$16/\\text{hour} = \\$240\n$$\n\n---\n\n### **Total Weekly Earnings:**\n\n$$\n\\$600 (\\tex