s a remainder. But here, 420 divided by 10 exactly gives 42 with no remainder. So there's no need for an extra page. So the answer should be 42. \n\nLet me make sure I didn't make any arithmetic mistakes. 60 packs, 7 per pack. 7 times 60. Let me verify another way. 7 times 60 is the same as 7 times 6 tens. 7 times 6 is 42, so 42 tens, which is 420. Yeah, that checks out. Then 420 divided by 10 is 42. Yep, that seems right. \n\nSo, the number of pages he needs to buy is 42. I think that's the answer. No need for any rounding or calculation here. It's exact division. So the final answer should be \\boxed{42}.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many pages Punger needs to buy, we first calculate the total number of baseball cards he collects. Each of the 60 packs contains 7 cards, and there are 60 packs:\n\n$$\n60 \\text{ packs} \\times 7 \\text{ cards per pack} = 420 \\text{ cards}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the number of pages required. Each page holds 10 cards, so the number of pages required is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{420 \\text{ cards}}{10 \\text{ cards per page}} = 42 \\text{ pages}\n$$\n\nSince the division results in an exact number with no remainder, there is no need for an extra page. Hence, the total number of pages Punger needs to buy is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{42}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can hold 10 cards. How many pages does he need to buy?", "answer": "60 packs of cards * 7 cards per pack = <<60*7=420>>420 cards\n420 cards / 10 cards per page = <<420/10=42>>42 pages\n#### 42", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Punger collects baseball cards.  He buys 60 packs of baseball cards. Each pack has 7 cards inside. He wants to put these cards in special pages to protect the cards. Each page can ho