ybe the problem is straightforward, and the answer is 15kg. So I think that's the answer.\n</think>\n\nTo determine how many kilograms of dog food Elise already had before buying the additional bags, we start by identifying the information provided:\n\n- She purchased two bags of dog food:\n  - 1st bag: $15\\ \\text{kg}$\n  - 2nd bag: $10\\ \\text{kg}$\n- She now has a total of $40\\ \\text{kg}$ of dog food.\n\nLet the amount of dog food Elise had **before** buying the new bags be $x$. Therefore, the total after buying both bags will be:\n\n$$\nx + 15 + 10 = 40\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$:\n\n$$\nx + 25 = 40 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = 40 - 25 = 15\n$$\n\n---\n\nSo, Elise had $\\boxed{15}$ kilograms of dog food before buying the additional bags.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Elise is buying more dog food. She buys a 15kg bag then another 10kg bag, and she now has 40kg of dog food. How many kilograms of dog food did Elise already have?", "answer": "Elise has bought a total of 15 kg + 10 kg = <<15+10=25>>25kg of dog food.\nThis means she already had 40 kg – 25 kg = <<40-25=15>>15kg of dog food.\n#### 15", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Elise is buying more dog food. She buys a 15kg bag then another 10kg bag, and she now has 40kg of dog food. How many kilograms of dog food did Elise already have?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["15"], "solution": "15"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 3984, 1064, 374, 11833, 803, 5562, 3607, 13, 2932, 49531, 264, 220, 16, 20, 7351, 8968, 1221, 2441, 220, 16, 15, 7351, 8968, 11, 323, 1340, 1431, 702, 220, 19, 15, 7351, 315, 5562, 3607, 13, 2585], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 3984, 1064, 374, 11833, 803, 5562, 3607, 13, 2932, 49531, 264, 220, 16, 20, 7351, 8968,