ether\" refers to only Cecile? But the way the problem is phrased, it says \"how many apples did they buy altogether,\" so they are both considering Diane and Cecile. Therefore, the total would indeed be 50. \n\nI don't see any other interpretations. Maybe I should present the answer as 50, boxed as requested.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{50}\n</think>\n\nTo solve the problem, we start by understanding how many apples Diane bought based on the information given.\n\n- Cecile bought **15 apples**.\n- Diane bought **twenty more apples** than Cecile, so we calculate Diane's apples as:\n  $$\n  15 + 20 = 35\n  $$\n\nNext, we determine the total number of apples bought by both Diane and Cecile:\n$$\n15 + 35 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of apples bought altogether is:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{50}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?", "answer": "Diane bought 15 + 20 = <<15+20=35>>35 apples.\nTherefore, they bought 15 + 35 = <<15+35=50>>50 apples altogether.\n#### 50", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Diane bought twenty more apples than Cecile. If Cecile bought 15 apples, how many apples did they buy altogether?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["50"], "solution": "50"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 13, 1416, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 11, 1246, 1657, 40676, 1521, 807, 3695, 30055, 5267, 36842, 25, 151645, 198, 151644, 77091, 198], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 773, 53090, 10788, 17073, 803, 40676, 1091, 49145, 457, 11, 323, 49145, 457, 10788, 220, 16, 20, 40676, 13, 576, 3405, 374, 10161, 1246, 1657, 40676, 807, 10788, 30055, 13, 6771, 752, 1744