hat 12. So 12 multiplied by 1/4 is 3. Then, total ceilings painted would be 12 (this week) plus 3 (next week) equals 15. Therefore, the number left to paint would be 28 - 15 = 13. Is that correct?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is when he paints 1/4 of the total number of ceilings he was going to paint, not necessarily the number he painted this week. But that seems unlikely. Let me check again. The problem says \"he paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week.\" So \"his\" is referring to the week before. So 1/4 of 12? So 12/4 is 3. Therefore, next week he paints 3 ceilings. So total painted is 12 + 3 = 15. Left is 28 - 15 = 13. So the answer is 13?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that he paints 1/4 of the total? For example, if the total ceilings he needs to paint is 28, then 1/4 of 28 is 7. But then he painted 12 this week. Then next week he paints 7, leaving 28 - 12 -7 = 9. But that would be if \"he does this week\" refers to the total. But the wording is \"paints 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week.\" So maybe he paints 1/4 of the 12 he did this week.\n\nBut that seems more direct. Let me verify. Maybe an example: If he painted 12 this week, and he paints 1/4 the number he did this week. So 12 times 1/4 is 3. So next week he paints 3. So total left is 28 - 15 = 13. That seems straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is because of some other interpretation. For example, sometimes in math problems, they can be ambiguous. For example, maybe \"he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week\" meaning that this week he painted 12, then next week he paints 1/4 of the tunnels he did this week (which is 12), so 3. So 3 is added. So I think that's the correct interpretation.\n\nTherefore, 28 - 12 - 3 = 13. So the answer should be 13, which is $\\boxed{13}$.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{13}\n</think>\n\nMichelangelo starts with a total of 28 ceilings to paint. First, he paints 12 of them this week. \n\nNext week, he paints **1/4** the number of ceilings he painted this week. Since he painted 12 this week, he paints $ \\frac{1}{4} \\times 12 = 3 $ ceilings next week.\n\nTo find how many ceilings will be left to paint after next week, we subtract the total painted ceilings from the original:\n\n$$\n28 - 12 - 3 = 13\n$$\n\nThus, the number of ceilings left to paint after next week is $\\boxed{13}$.<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?", "answer": "After this week, Michelangelo will have 28-12=<<28-12=16>>16 more ceilings to paint.\nNext week, Michelangelo will paint 12/4=<<12/4=3>>3 ceilings.\nMichelangelo will have 16-3=<<16-3=13>>13 more ceilings to paint.\n#### 13", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Michelangelo has 28 ceilings to paint. This week, he paints 12 of them. Next week, he will paint 1/4 the number of ceilings he did this week. How many ceilings will be left to paint after next week?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["13"], "solution": "13"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 9295, 2003, 11, 566, 686, 6177, 220, 16, 14, 19, 279, 1372, 315, 55714, 566, 1521], "response_tokens": [151667, 198, 32313, 11, 1077, 594, 1490, 13, 44406, 87910, 702, 220, 17, 23, 55714, 311, 6177, 304, 2790, 13, 1096, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 17, 315, 1105, 13, 5005, 1790, 2003, 11, 566, 56848, 220, 16, 14, 19, 315, 279, 1372, 566, 1521, 419, 2003, 13, 358, 1184, 311, 7071, 700, 1246, 1657, 55714, 686, 387, 2115, 1283, 1790, 2003, 13]}
