 320 is 96000? Wait, no, 320 * 30. 30 * 300 is 9000, and 30 * 20 is 600, so total is 9600. Yes, that's right. So 9600 dollars in total?\n\nBut hold on, they repeated the same performance two more days. So does that mean they did the performance on three days? Let me see. The problem says \"they repeated the same performance 2 other days\". So the first day they had the performance, and then two more days. Therefore, total days is 1 + 2 = 3 days? So total tickets would be 3 * 320 * 30? Wait, is that correct?\n\nWait, hold on. If they have 320 seats each day, and they perform 2 days, so each of those two days, 320 * 30 tickets sold. Then total is 2 * 320 * 30? Or is it 1 day times 320 *30? Let me read again.\n\n\"A small theater company sells tickets to a show. They have a 400 seat theater and fill to 80% capacity. Each ticket cost $30. They repeated the same performance 2 other days. How much did they make?\"\n\nSo, \"they repeated 2 other days\". So, starting from the first day, then two more days. So total days is 3. Therefore, total tickets sold would be 3 days * 320 *30?\n\nYes, so total revenue is 3 * 320 * 30. Let me calculate that again. 3 * 320 is 960, then 960 * 30 is 28800 dollars. Wait, but hold on, earlier I thought it's 3 days, but that would be 3*320*30. But if the first day is considered as time 1, then two more days would be time 2 and 3, so three days. Wait, but maybe the problem is considering 2 days? Let me check.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"They repeated the same performance 2 other days.\" So, does that mean that they started the performance on day 1, then repeated it on day 2 and 3, so three days? Or did they only perform the same performance once and then have two more days? Maybe there's ambiguity here.\n\nWait, let's re-read:\n\n\"A small theater company sells tickets to a show. They have a 400 seat theater and fill to 80% capacity. Each ticket cost $30. They repeated the same performance 2 other days. How much did they make?\"\n\nSo, \"They