tal slices would be 3*10 + 1*15. Which is 45. \n\nIs there anything else I missed? Maybe the watermelons are cut in different ways, but the problem says they were cut into 10 and 15 slices. So no, the answer should be 45. \n\nJust to be thorough, maybe convert them into decimals? 3*10 is 30, 1*15 is 15. Total 45. Yep. \n\nSo I think the answer is 45.\n</think>\n\nDanny brings 3 watermelons to the picnic. Each watermelon, when cut, is divided into 10 slices. Therefore, Danny contributes:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 10 = 30 \\text{ watermelon slices}\n$$\n\nHis sister brings 1 watermelon, which is cut into 15 slices. Thus, her contribution is:\n\n$$\n1 \\times 15 = 15 \\text{ watermelon slices}\n$$\n\nThe total number of watermelon slices at the picnic is:\n\n$$\n30 + 15 = \\boxed{45}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Danny brings 3 watermelons to his family picnic. He cuts each watermelon into 10 slices. His sister brings 1 watermelon to the family picnic, and she cuts the watermelon into 15 slices. How many watermelon slices are there in total at the picnic?", "answer": "From Danny, there are 3 * 10 = <<3*10=30>>30 watermelon slices\nFrom his sister, there are 1 * 15 = <<1*15=15>>15 watermelon slices\nThere are a total of 30 + 15 = <<30+15=45>>45 watermelon slices\n#### 45", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Danny brings 3 watermelons to his family picnic. He cuts each watermelon into 10 slices. His sister brings 1 watermelon to the family picnic, and she cuts the watermelon into 15 slices. How many watermelon slices are there in total at the picnic?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["45"], "solution": "45"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 32599, 12434, 220, 18, 3015, 27127, 2382, 311, 806, 2997, 54462, 13, 1260, 15104, 1817, 3015, 71118,