rward. Kaylee needs 33, the totals so far are 21, so 33 - 21 = 12. Yep, seems right. Maybe I should present that as the answer. \n\nJust to make sure, maybe the question is worded differently. It says \"how many more boxes does Kaylee need to sell.\" So yeah, it's 33 - 21 = 12. So the answer is 12. I think that's all. No step-by-step errors here. Okay, I think that's it.\n</think>\n\nKaylee needs to sell a total of 33 boxes of biscuits. She has already sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to her neighbor. \n\nFirst, we sum up the boxes already sold:\n$$ 12 + 5 + 4 = 21 $$\n\nNext, we determine how many more boxes she needs to sell:\n$$ 33 - 21 = 12 $$\n\nTherefore, Kaylee needs to sell **12 more boxes of biscuits**. \n\n$$\n\\boxed{12}\n$$<|im_end|>", "source_record": {"question": "Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?", "answer": "The number of boxes already sold is 12 + 5 + 4 = <<12+5+4=21>>21.\nKaylee needs to sell 33 − 21 = 12 more boxes.\n#### 12", "prompt": "Solve the following grade-school math problem. Show your reasoning and put the final answer in \\boxed{}.\n\nProblem: Kaylee needs to sell 33 boxes of biscuits. So far, she has sold 12 boxes of lemon biscuits to her aunt, 5 boxes of chocolate biscuits to her mother, and 4 boxes of oatmeal biscuits to a neighbor. How many more boxes of biscuits does Kaylee need to sell?\nSolution:", "solutions": ["12"], "solution": "12"}, "prompt_tokens": [151644, 872, 198, 50, 3948, 279, 2701, 11972, 34689, 6888, 3491, 13, 6928, 697, 32711, 323, 2182, 279, 1590, 4226, 304, 1124, 79075, 6257, 382, 31198, 25, 30145, 8501, 3880, 311, 4559, 220, 18, 18, 14697, 315, 85976, 13, 2055, 3041, 11, 1340, 702, 6088, 220, 16, 17, 14697, 315, 29464, 85976, 311, 1